knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.width = 6, fig.height = 4,
                      message = FALSE)
has_aer <- requireNamespace("AER", quietly = TRUE)
```

## The problem

Many outcomes in the social sciences are unordered choices among several
alternatives: a vote for one of several candidates or for none, a travel mode,
a type of political action, a treaty design. The workhorse model is the
multinomial logit (Theil 1969; McFadden 1974), which comes in two notations
that `polychoice` treats as one model:

* the **multinomial logit** of the wide layout, one row per case, with
  case-specific covariates whose coefficients differ by alternative
  (`y ~ x1 + x2`);
* the **conditional logit** of the long layout, one row per case and
  alternative, with alternative-specific covariates whose coefficients are
  common to all alternatives (`chosen ~ w | x`, with `id` and `alt`).

Both are the same choice probability

\[
P(y_i = j) = \frac{a_{ij} \exp(V_{ij})}{\sum_k a_{ik} \exp(V_{ik})}, \qquad
V_{ij} = o_{ij} + x_i'\beta_j + w_{ij}'\gamma,
\]

with \(a_{ij}\) indicating whether alternative \(j\) is available to case
\(i\), \(\beta_b = 0\) for the base alternative, and an offset \(o_{ij}\).

The model's convenience comes with the independence of irrelevant alternatives
(IIA): the odds between any two alternatives do not depend on the others.
`polychoice` provides the estimators that relax IIA (nested logit,
heteroskedastic extreme value, multinomial probit, random intercepts), the
baseline-inflated multinomial logit of Bagozzi and Marchetti (2017) for a base
alternative that mixes two kinds of cases, and the full battery of tests of IIA
and of specification, all behind one interface. This vignette covers the
interface; the companion vignettes cover the inflated model, the tests, and
the models beyond IIA.

## One interface, seven estimators

| Function      | Model                                                          |
|---------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
| `mnl()`       | multinomial / conditional logit                                |
| `imnl()`      | inflated multinomial logit (one or several inflated alternatives) |
| `nlogit()`    | two-level nested logit                                         |
| `hev()`       | heteroskedastic extreme value                                  |
| `mnprobit()`  | multinomial probit with a free covariance                      |
| `remnl()`     | multinomial logit with random alternative-specific intercepts  |
| `rologit()`   | rank-ordered (exploded) logit for rankings                     |

Every estimator takes the same arguments for the data layout (`id`, `alt`),
the base alternative (`ref`), frequency `weights`, an `offset`, unit fixed
effects (`fe`; not `imnl()`, whose split equation they leave identified only
through the functional form, nor `remnl()`, whose unit effects are the random
intercepts, nor `rologit()`),
and standard errors (`se`: inverse observed information,
outer product of the scores, robust, cluster-robust, or bootstrap), and
returns an object of class `"choicemodel"` with the usual methods (`summary`,
`coef`, `vcov`, `predict`, `confint`, `logLik`, `update`, `simulate`) plus
`broom` tidiers and `texreg` / `modelsummary` support.

## A first fit: the 2006 Pennsylvania state House vote

`pa2006` holds the survey of Arceneaux and Kolodny (2009) after a field
experiment in which an interest group endorsed the Democratic candidate by
canvassing or by telephone. The outcome has four alternatives, with abstention
as the base.

```{r pa2006}
library(polychoice)
data(pa2006)
table(pa2006$vote)
m <- mnl(vote ~ canvass + phone + democrat + republican + age + female + voted2004,
         data = pa2006)
summary(m)
```

The coefficient names are `alternative:term`: each non-base alternative has
its own equation against abstention. `rrr()` reports relative risk ratios
(exponentiated coefficients) with confidence intervals; `summary()` also
shows the information criteria and the standard-error type.

```{r rrr}
head(rrr(m), 6)
```

### Predicted probabilities and first differences

`predict()` returns the probability of every alternative for the estimation
rows or for `newdata`; `type = "class"` picks the most likely alternative.

```{r predict}
head(predict(m), 3)
nd <- data.frame(canvass = c(0, 1), phone = 0, democrat = 1, republican = 0, age = 45,
                 female = 1, voted2004 = 1)
predict(m, newdata = nd)
```

`first_difference()` is the quantity of interest most analyses report: the
change in every alternative's probability when one covariate moves between two
values. By default in the wide layout the other covariates are held at a
profile of their weighted means and modes; `newdata` gives another one-case
profile; `average = TRUE` (the default in the long layout) averages the first
difference over the estimation cases, the average effect many applied
reports prefer to the effect at a profile.
The columns `from` and `to` are the two predicted probabilities. Delta-method
intervals are the default; `ci = "sim"` draws from the asymptotic normal
distribution of the estimates instead.

```{r fd}
first_difference(m, "canvass", from = 0, to = 1)
first_difference(m, "canvass", from = 0, to = 1, average = TRUE)
```

### Fit and classification

`fit_stats()` gives the log-likelihood, information criteria, and the
pseudo-\(R^2\) measures in common use (all relative to the null model with
alternative-specific constants only); `classification()` cross-tabulates the
observed and predicted alternatives.

```{r fit}
fit_stats(m)
classification(m)
```

## The long layout: alternative-specific covariates

When covariates vary across alternatives (travel time, price, distance to a
polling place), the data come as one row per case and alternative and the
formula has two parts: `chosen ~ w | x`, where `w` are the alternative-specific
covariates with a common coefficient and `x` the case-specific ones with
alternative-specific coefficients. The intercept of the first part switches
the alternative-specific constants on (the default); `chosen ~ 0 + w | x`
switches them off. The classic example is the Australian travel mode data
distributed with the `AER` package.

```{r long, eval = requireNamespace("AER", quietly = TRUE)}
data("TravelMode", package = "AER")
TM <- TravelMode
TM$chosen <- as.integer(TM$choice == "yes")
head(TM, 4)
mc <- mnl(chosen ~ wait + vcost + travel + gcost | income + size, data = TM,
          id = "individual", alt = "mode")
summary(mc)
```

A covariate in the first part can be given an alternative-specific
coefficient with `w:alt` (for example `vcost:mode`, a cost coefficient per mode), so no third formula part is
needed. Alternatives absent from a case's rows are treated as unavailable
to that case. This is also how a stated consideration set enters: when a
survey asks which parties a respondent would consider voting for, keeping
only those parties' rows makes `mnl()` the model of the choice within the
considered set (which parties are considered is then a separate set of binary
models; Oscarsson and Rosema 2019 recommend such direct measures). A
consideration set inferred from the choices alone, without such a question,
is weakly identified in the designs political scientists have: the package's
verification found the standard errors of the consideration parameters as
large as the parameters at 2,000 cases with respondent characteristics alone,
recovery failing when the same voter-party distances drive both stages, and,
even with a distance that shapes consideration only, intervals that covered
0.88-0.91 instead of 0.95 in a realistic design. The package does not fit it.

## Rankings

When respondents rank the alternatives instead of choosing one (a survey that
asks voters to order the parties, the ballots of a ranked-choice election),
`rologit()` fits the rank-ordered logit (Beggs, Cardell and Hausman 1981),
also called the exploded logit after Chapman and Staelin's (1982) "explosion"
of a ranking into independent choices. A ranking is read as a sequence of
multinomial-logit choices: the first-ranked alternative from the whole choice
set, the second from those left, and so on. Partial rankings (only the first
few positions) and tied ranks are handled exactly (Allison and Christakis
1994). The data are in the long
layout with the rank as the response, smaller preferred and 0 for an
alternative left unranked; `rrologit()` simulates such data.

```{r rologit}
set.seed(8)
dk <- rrologit(800, beta = cbind(b = c(0.5, -1, 0.8), c = c(-0.3, 0.6, -0.5), d = c(0.2, 0.4, 0.3)),
               gamma = 0.7, depth = 3)
head(dk, 4)
mro <- rologit(rank ~ w1 | x1 + x2, data = dk, id = "id", alt = "alt")
mfc <- rologit(rank ~ w1 | x1 + x2, data = dk, id = "id", alt = "alt", depth = 1)   # first choices only
cbind(rankings = sqrt(diag(vcov(mro))), first_choices = sqrt(diag(vcov(mfc))))[c(1:3, 10), ]
```

The rankings estimate the same coefficients as the first choices with
smaller standard errors. The model assumes that preferences are the same at
every stage of the ranking; `rank_test()` refits with separate coefficients
for the stages after the first and tests the difference (Hausman and Ruud
1987, section 3; Allison and Christakis 1994, section 5.4):

```{r rank-test}
rank_test(mro, after = 1)
```

## Standard errors, weights, and panels

`se = "robust"` gives the sandwich over cases; `cluster = "<cluster column>"` the
cluster-robust version (and selects `se = "cluster"`); `se = "bootstrap"`
resamples cases, or clusters when `cluster` is given (units with unit fixed
effects or random intercepts), with `nboot` refits.
`weights` are frequency weights: a case with weight 2 counts as two
identical cases in the estimates, the model-based standard errors, the
information criteria, and the averaged quantities of interest. Survey
(probability) weights are not supported: `se = "robust"` is the
frequency-weight sandwich, not a design-based variance (see `?mnl`).

```{r robust}
mr <- mnl(vote ~ canvass + phone + democrat + republican + age + female + voted2004,
          data = pa2006, se = "robust")
cbind(analytic = sqrt(diag(vcov(m)))[1:4], robust = sqrt(diag(vcov(mr)))[1:4])
```

For panels, `fe = "<unit column>"` (the name of the column that identifies
the units) adds alternative-specific constants per unit (with
the never-chosen rule and, optionally, `bias_correct = "jackknife"`),
`remnl()` integrates out random alternative-specific intercepts, and
`mundlak()` adds unit means of the covariates for a correlated random-effects
specification. These are covered in the vignette *Beyond IIA*.

## Reporting

`broom::tidy()` and `broom::glance()` work on every fit, and `texreg` and
`modelsummary` tables can be built directly from the fitted objects.

```{r broom, eval = requireNamespace("broom", quietly = TRUE)}
head(broom::tidy(m, conf.int = TRUE), 4)
broom::glance(m)
```

With multiply imputed data, fit the model on each completed data set and
pass the list of fits to `mice::pool()`, which combines them by Rubin's
rules through the same methods. The estimators take the data as an
argument, so `with(imp, mnl(...))` does not work. The fits' flags are not
pooled, so check each fit first. And give `pool()` the complete-data
degrees of freedom, cases minus parameters: left to itself, mice 3.19
counts one residual per case and alternative. The pooled statistic tests
each parameter against zero (`?tidy.choicemodel`).

```{r mice, eval = requireNamespace("mice", quietly = TRUE)}
set.seed(1)
dm <- rmnl(500, beta = cbind(b = c(0.5, -1, 0.8), c = c(-0.3, 0.6, -0.5)))
dm$x1[sample(500, 80)] <- NA
imp <- mice::mice(dm, m = 5, printFlag = FALSE, seed = 1)
fits <- lapply(1:5, function(i) mnl(y ~ x1 + x2, data = mice::complete(imp, i)))
all(vapply(fits, function(f) f$converged && !f$boundary && !f$singular, logical(1)))
pooled <- mice::pool(fits, dfcom = nobs(fits[[1]]) - length(coef(fits[[1]])))
summary(pooled)[, c("term", "estimate", "std.error", "statistic", "df", "p.value")]
```

## Where next

* *The inflated multinomial logit* fits the model of Bagozzi and
  Marchetti (2017) and reproduces the published applications.
* *Testing IIA and specification* covers `iia_tests()` and the further tests,
  with the size study that motivates the recommendations.
* *Beyond IIA* covers the nested logit, HEV, multinomial probit, random intercepts, and
  the fixed-effects tools.
* *Seven published analyses* works through the example datasets drawn from
  replication archives (`sanctions`, `fluvax`, `bes1987`, `defection`,
  `bes2010`, `cabinets`, `afghan_leaders`), four of them reproducing their
  published models, one of them with Firth's penalized likelihood under
  separation.

## References

Allison, P.D. and Christakis, N.A. (1994). Logit models for sets of ranked
items. *Sociological Methodology*, 24, 199-228.

Arceneaux, K. and Kolodny, R. (2009). Educating the least informed: Group
endorsements in a grassroots campaign. *American Journal of Political
Science*, 53, 755-770.

Bagozzi, B.E. and Marchetti, K. (2017). Distinguishing occasional abstention
from routine indifference in models of vote choice. *Political Science
Research and Methods*, 5(2), 277-294.

Beggs, S., Cardell, S. and Hausman, J. (1981). Assessing the potential demand
for electric cars. *Journal of Econometrics*, 17, 1-19.

Chapman, R.G. and Staelin, R. (1982). Exploiting rank ordered choice set data
within the stochastic utility model. *Journal of Marketing Research*, 19,
288-301.

Hausman, J.A. and Ruud, P.A. (1987). Specifying and testing econometric models
for rank-ordered data. *Journal of Econometrics*, 34, 83-104.

McFadden, D. (1974). Conditional logit analysis of qualitative choice
behavior. In P. Zarembka (ed.), *Frontiers in Econometrics*, 105-142.
Academic Press.

Oscarsson, H. and Rosema, M. (2019). Consideration set models of electoral
choice: Theory, method, and application. *Electoral Studies*, 57, 256-262.

Theil, H. (1969). A multinomial extension of the linear logit model.
*International Economic Review*, 10, 251-259.
